## Universal CLI tools every strain needs, GUI or headless. No desktop
## environment here on purpose — DE choice (or none, for server/cloud) is
## a per-strain decision, see iso/strains/*.list.chroot and iso/build.sh's
## --strain handling. (Previously this file had NO desktop environment
## package anywhere, which meant the original skeleton would have produced
## a GUI-less image regardless of strain -- caught and fixed while adding
## strain selection, see TODO.md.)
curl
jq
git
## build-essential + cmake are deliberately NOT baked in (~110-140 MB installed;
## part of keeping every ISO under GitHub's 2 GiB single-file cap). Nothing in
## the shipped image needs a compiler at runtime: the only repo consumer,
## modes/ai/legacy-crucible12/setup/01-install-llamacpp.sh, apt-installs them
## itself on demand, and hooks/0410-keyd.chroot self-installs gcc/make at build
## time and purges them again. Don't re-add here — apt-get install works fine
## on an installed system when a user actually wants a toolchain.
linux-tools-common
## Must match the HWE kernel build.sh installs (--linux-flavours
## generic-hwe-24.04), NOT the GA kernel. linux-tools-common ships the
## /usr/bin/cpupower|perf wrappers that exec /usr/lib/linux-tools/$(uname -r)/<tool>;
## the GA linux-tools-generic never installs the HWE-ABI directory, so cpupower
## (used by distro-modectl for every performance mode) would fail at runtime
## once the HWE ABI diverges from GA. The -hwe-24.04 tools track the HWE ABI.
linux-tools-generic-hwe-24.04
power-profiles-daemon
mokutil
ffmpeg
## openssh-server is deliberately NOT in this universal base list -- it would
## expose an sshd on :22 on every desktop install (laptop/workstation/handheld/
## lowspec) that never asked for one. It now ships only in the strains that want
## it: iso/strains/server.list.chroot and iso/strains/cloud.list.chroot. Don't
## re-add it here.
## Explicit device firmware (WiFi/GPU/Bluetooth blobs). Almost always pulled in
## transitively by the HWE kernel + ubuntu-desktop-minimal, but pinned here so a
## first flash never boots with a dead Intel iwlwifi radio just because a
## transitive Recommends got dropped. Belt-and-suspenders, no downside.
linux-firmware
## rsync — REQUIRED BY THE INSTALLER, and its absence made installing impossible.
## Calamares' unpackfs module shells out to rsync to copy
## /cdrom/casper/filesystem.squashfs onto the freshly-partitioned target. rsync
## was in no package list and nothing pulled it in transitively, so the job died
## with exit 127 (command not found) and the installer showed:
##     Installation Failed
##     Failed to unpack image "/cdrom/casper/filesystem.squashfs"
##     Details: rsync failed with error code 127.
## Captured from install-smoke run 31519214084 — the first run to get far enough
## to reach unpackfs at all. Partitioning had already succeeded (the target disk
## grew from 200704 to 331776 bytes, i.e. a partition table was written), so
## this was the very next step and the last thing standing between the wizard
## and a real install.
rsync
## squashfs-tools — unpackfs handles a squashfs source by mounting it and, in
## some code paths, by calling unsquashfs. Pinned alongside rsync so the same
## class of silent exit-127 cannot bite on the other branch.
squashfs-tools
## ufw — the image shipped with NO firewall of any kind (measured: ufw and
## firewalld both absent from the built image). That is indefensible on the
## server and cloud strains, which install openssh-server and expose :22 to the
## network. Ubuntu's standard, open source, ~1 MB. Configured and enabled by
## iso/config/hooks/0460-firewall.chroot, which allows SSH FIRST on any strain
## that ships sshd — enabling a default-deny firewall without that rule would
## lock the owner out of their own server on first boot.
ufw
## cryptsetup + cryptsetup-initramfs — the installer OFFERS full-disk encryption
## ("Encrypt system" on the Calamares partition page, with luksGeneration: luks1
## in partition.conf) but cryptsetup was NOT in the image. Anyone ticking that
## box got a failed install, or an installed system with no way to unlock its own
## root at boot. Measured, not guessed: verify-boot-fixes' installer-toolchain
## check reported "installer tool MISSING: cryptsetup" while every other
## partition tool (mkfs.ext4/vfat/btrfs, mkswap, parted, sgdisk, wipefs) passed.
## cryptsetup-initramfs is the half people forget — without it the initramfs has
## no unlock hook and an encrypted install boots to a dead prompt.
cryptsetup
cryptsetup-initramfs
## GRUB TARGET MODULES — the installed system's bootloader, and the reason the
## first real hardware install failed at the last step. Calamares' bootloader
## module runs, inside the target:
##     grub-install --target=x86_64-efi --efi-directory=/boot/efi \
##                  --bootloader-id=ubuntu --force
## which reads its platform modules from /usr/lib/grub/x86_64-efi/. That
## directory ships in grub-efi-amd64-bin, which was in NO package list, so the
## command exited 1 and the install died after everything else had succeeded:
##     "Bootloader installation error ... returned error code 1"
## (photographed on a ThinkPad UEFI install, 2026-08-12).
##
## live-build installs grub to boot the ISO ITSELF; that is a different thing
## from the target's grub, and it left no x86_64-efi module tree in the squashfs.
##
## Both platforms are pinned, because one image installs onto both kinds of
## machine: -efi for UEFI, grub-pc-bin for BIOS/CSM. The *-bin packages are the
## module trees only and coexist happily — it is the full grub-pc and
## grub-efi-amd64 packages that conflict, so do NOT "simplify" this by swapping
## a -bin for its parent.
grub-efi-amd64-bin
grub-pc-bin
grub2-common
grub-common
## Signed chain for Secure Boot. Without these, a UEFI machine with Secure Boot
## left ON installs a bootloader its firmware then refuses to run — an install
## that reports success and produces an unbootable disk.
grub-efi-amd64-signed
shim-signed
## efibootmgr writes the UEFI boot entry; bootloader.conf names it explicitly
## (efiBootMgr: "efibootmgr") and grub-install shells out to it on EFI systems.
efibootmgr
